  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 137 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nScattered convection has developed across the mountains this\nafternoon, with the plume of deeper monsoon moisture working its\nway into the forecast area. Most of this convection so far has\nbeen fairly weak, which isn't much of a surprise with only modest\ninstability noted on SPC Mesoanalysis. Scattered storms should\ncontinue through the next several hours before diminishing after\nsunset. \n\nMeanwhile, it's a different story across most of the metro. A few\nweak showers have cooled temperatures in the Boulder area, but\ntemperatures across the metro have warmed into the low to mid 90s\n(currently 94F at DEN), with dew points still sitting in the low\nto mid 40s in many locations. Unsurprisingly, this has left a\nmajority of the urban corridor with ~250 J/kg of MLCAPE, but also\n150 J/kg of MLCIN. It's going to be difficult to get much of any\nmeaningful precipitation given that sort of setup, which has been\nconsistently advertised by CAMs over the last 24 hours or so.\n\nFinally, there's a well defined moisture gradient across the\neastern plains, with surface dew points in the low to mid 60s\nalong and east of a line from roughly Sterling to Burlington.\nGuidance eventually tries to strengthen the existing cluster of\nweak convection over the Palmer Divide once it hits the more\nfavorable environment in far east/northeastern Colorado later this\nafternoon. Forecast soundings would imply both a marginal\nhail/wind risk through tonight despite a relatively uncertain\nconvective evolution.\n\nAs we get into tomorrow, moisture is expected to increase across\nmost of the forecast area, with PW standardized anomalies close \nto 2-3 sigma (roughly about 125-200% of normal). Despite the \nrelatively favorable environment featuring moderate buoyancy and \ncontinued upslope flow, there are still many questions \nsurrounding convective evolution - particularly across the metro -\nthat lead to low confidence in our PoP/QPF forecasts. While there\nis still some subtle synoptic support, the overnight cold front \nand/or outflow boundary is not well-defined. For most folks along \nthe I-25 corridor, rainfall chances will likely be very dependent \non favorable mesoscale influences that are generally not well \nrepresented by CAMs. Any storms that do develop will be relatively\nslow moving and capable of producing heavy rain, but it's \ndifficult to come up with the \"where\" part of the forecast here. \nUnderstandably there is a lot of spread in the HREF/REFS at this \ntime which will hopefully be better resolved once we get through \ntoday's convection.\n\nOur cooler and unsettled pattern is likely to continue through the\nweekend here with above normal moisture sticking around. Again\nthere are mixed signal across guidance during most of this\nperiod. A few models try to push a weak front through at some\npoint, while others are less bullish. With meager synoptic forcing\nto work with, heavier rainfall potential would generally favor the\nFront Range mountains and foothills, with lower chances again\nacross the metro. NBM PoPs and QPF are far more optimistic than I\nam during this period, and while I did bring down both PoP/QPF for\nthe weekend, I wouldn't be surprised if we make further\nadjustments over the next couple days.\n\nRidging is expected to slowly build back towards our area by early\nnext week, which should gradually push the deeper moisture plume\nout of the forecast area. It doesn't look nearly as warm or dry as\nit did to start the month... and extended range ensemble guidance\nstill favors a return of moisture during the latter half of the\nweek.",
